<<~ ahu slot=`#/body`>>

Terra-Session-Reader: Lares (Tasked Spirit). A blind cold read, read-only, custody-adjacent, of B1 slice 3 (parts i and ii).
Read only `git -C /home/joshu/.lares-scratch/b1-lift/wt diff d70dd8196 39c767601` and `git -C ... show 39c767601:<path>` (a builder writes in that tree; never read its working files). Design: `show 39c767601:bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/browser-vessel-lift.mem` (slice 3), `bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract.mem`, `bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem`.
Claims: each tab's port opens an in-memory session (`vessel-sessions.ts`), admitted only by its own unlock; the face's last admitted session closing (pagehide, port loss, the house's own liveness budget) ends acts in flight, flushes, closes the vessel, zero-fills the tree key; one owner per store (Web Lock, `store-owned`, no steal); a respawn reissues `ownerEpoch` and refuses stale ids; per-face custody keys (FCK) and slot trees in `browser-face-custody.ts`; `face` on every ask and clear; one provider per face; booking by request id; sibling sealing; the ember row; `no-custody-slot`; the face-switch fence hooks.
Hunt: a session that reads or signs without its own unlock; a key that survives the last session's close (any copy, cache, closure, worker); abrupt-loss detection that misses a case or uses a cross-process clock; a second worker or tab that takes the store; one face's session reading another face's rows (the K15 isolation) on any path; a faceless ask or clear accepted; sibling sealing across faces; test seams reachable from product code.
Output (final message, under 600 words): findings P1/P2/P3 with file:line, MEASURED vs INFER; verdict LAND / LAND-AFTER-CURES / HOLD.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/seat-law`>>

!! SEAT LAW (appended by tools/launch-codex.mjs to every brief; it binds this seat)
* A lane launches no Sol seat; the controller's one Sol read per train runs at the read floor of 0%.
* Stay inside the wall. Approval never escalates; a build seat has no network, loopback included; only a research seat takes `--search`. A step that needs more than the wall stops and names itself in `out.md`; the wall holds by refusal.
* Never commit and never run a git write. The lead commits each red, then each fix.
* Never build (`pnpm build`), never run vitest browser mode, never stand a vessel, socket or daemon. Typecheck (`tsc -p … --noEmit`) and run single test files that open no socket; mark any test that needs a socket "lead runs".
* A "stale build" from the dist guard stops the run; name it, and the lead builds.
* A read seat writes no file: its final message carries the hand-back. Any other seat writes `out.md` and every note in the seat directory alone. `-o` names `final.md`; stdin closes (`< /dev/null`); `TMPDIR` and `TMPPREFIX` sit inside the seat directory.
* Never run the battery; the lead holds its lock. Never read a credential file.
* Launch without `--ephemeral`: each seat keeps its rollout, so the spend tally counts it.
* The lead runs `pnpm build` in its worktree after every span merge and before each build seat; a verdict read off a stale dist counts as unread.

<<~/ahu>>

